# Explore the Hidden Size of Search

A candidate derivation is a path through a finite tree. Start with the interactive explorer and see how **depth** and **branching** jointly control the number of possible paths.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The exact law is simple: a depth-$L$ word over $q$ symbols has $q^L$ possibilities and $L\log_2q$ bits of uniform indexing information. Compare fixed binary branching with branching that grows with $n$.

{{visualization:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the counting argument</summary>

Each of the $L$ positions independently offers $q$ symbols. The multiplication principle gives $q\cdot q\cdots q=q^L$. Taking a base-two logarithm turns the product into $L\log_2q$. In the special model $q=L=n$, this becomes exactly $n\log_2n$.

For background, see [the rule of product](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rule_of_product) and [self-information](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Information_content).
</details>

## Can clever notation shorten everything?

Use the compression lab to compare the $2^n$ binary objects of length $n$ with every binary description of length below $n$.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:1}}

{{algorithm:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the incompressibility theorem</summary>

There are $1+2+\cdots+2^{n-1}=2^n-1$ shorter descriptions, but $2^n$ objects. By the [pigeonhole principle](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pigeonhole_principle), no injective lossless encoding can strictly shorten every object. Individual objects may still compress; the impossibility concerns a uniform guarantee over all objects.
</details>

{{demo:1}}

## Why a hidden success may force exhaustive search

Imagine that validation answers only “yes” or “no.” Every unqueried location can still contain a unique success.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

{{algorithm:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the adversarial proof</summary>

After fewer than $N$ distinct negative queries, the queried set is a proper subset of the $N$ candidates. Choose any unqueried candidate as the secret. All observed answers remain negative both when no success exists and when this secret is the unique success. Therefore an exact deterministic decision can require all $N$ queries.
</details>

{{demo:2}}

## Beyond uniform trees

Real search trees need not branch uniformly. Multiplication and logarithmic additivity still provide the right accounting language.

{{algorithm:1}}

{{demo:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal composition and asymptotic structure</summary>

Independent families of sizes $N_1$ and $N_2$ combine into $N_1N_2$ pairs, while their information adds: $\log_2(N_1N_2)=\log_2N_1+\log_2N_2$. More generally, level-dependent branching $b_1,\ldots,b_L$ gives population $\prod_i b_i$ and information $\sum_i\log_2b_i$. See [subadditivity](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Subadditivity) for the asymptotic framework suggested by logarithmic candidate counts.
</details>

The baseline is deliberately strict: it measures search with no exploitable structure. Algebra, semantics, symmetry, and learned guidance can outperform enumeration precisely because they reveal information about many candidates at once.
